course: dictionary
lesson: 2335
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives crumble four meanings, and the words around it tell you which one to pick. «Mia rubbed butter and flour together to make the crumble topping.» The words about cooking and baking tell you that crumble here is a pudding.
explain: «The ground at the edge crumbled and he fell.» The ground was not baked or cut up. It broke into pieces and fell away, so crumble means to break into small pieces or to slowly fall apart. Ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Crumble can be a verb (a doing word) or a noun (a naming word). «Dry soil crumbles in your hand» uses crumble as a verb. «We ate an apple crumble with custard» uses it as a noun. Look at the words around it to see which job it does.
fact: To crumble is to break into small pieces.
fact: To crumble something is to break it into small pieces with your fingers.
fact: A crumble is a baked pudding of fruit covered with a crumbly mix of flour, butter and sugar.
fact: To crumble can also mean to slowly fall apart or fail.
fact: The verb forms are crumble, crumbles, crumbled and crumbling.
exercise: «The rock crumbled in the rain over many years.» What does crumbled mean here? | Shouted loudly | Turned into a bird | Baked in an oven | *Broke into small pieces slowly
exercise: Pupils rub flour and butter into a bowl to make an apple crumble. What is a crumble in this sentence? | *A baked pudding of fruit under a crumbly topping | A kind of cloud | A tool for cutting paper | A small rock
exercise: «Stay well back from cliff edges, because edges can crumble.» What does crumble mean here? | Shine brightly in the sun | Grow long green shoots | *Slowly break and fall apart | Sing a song loudly
test: «Dry soil crumbles when you touch it.» What job does crumbles do in this sentence? | *A doing word (verb) | A describing word (adjective) | A naming word (noun) | A joining word (conjunction)
test: «The old castle walls crumbled.» What does this most likely tell you? | The walls were washed clean | The walls were baked for a pudding | The walls grew taller | *The walls slowly broke and fell apart
exam: Which sentence uses crumble as a noun? | *We ate warm apple crumble with custard. | She crumbled the cheese over the salad. | The cliff may crumble after the storm. | The biscuit began to crumble in my hand.
